so hello everyone and welcome to my talk  on data transformation and exploratory  analysis I'd like to start off here by  asking you guys a few questions so first  how many of you consider yourselves data  scientists okay how many of you spend a  lot of time exploring data great and the  third question how many of you have a  formal process or framework that you use  for doing data exploration okay  excellent you've all come to the right  place  so what I'd like to do in this talk is  tell you a little bit about the process  that I use for exploratory data analysis  in my day to day work and for the few of  you that had a process of your own my  hope is that you see some some  similarities between my approach and  yours and that you take away a couple of  ideas of things that you can incorporate  into your own process and for those of  you that don't already have a process of  your own what I hope you take away from  this talk is an intuitive repeatable  framework for analyzing visualizing and  finding insights in your data but before  I jump into that I'd like to tell you a  little bit about myself so that's me my  name is Tony Ojeda I've been in the  analytics space and more recently in the  data science space for a little over a  decade now I'm the founder of district  data labs which is a data science  research lab and open source  collaborative based in Washington DC and  I got into data science from kind of a  business analytics and Finance  background which is where I acquired my  analytical and quantitative skills and  then I taught myself how to program over  the years in both R and Python and so in  the years that I've been in this field  I've worked with my fair share of  analyst data scientists as well students  learning data science and I've noticed  that particularly during the data  exploration phase of their work I often  see them grasping at straws when it  comes to their approach to finding  insights in the data and so I wanted to  put this presentation together to help  create a more structured path for people  so that they could follow it and view  insight  discovery has a problem break that  problem down into manageable components  and then work toward a solution and so  in this talk we're going to look at an  example of a real data set I'm going to  introduce you to the framework that I  use and then we're going to walk through  the concepts in the framework and use  them on the example data set but before  I do that and jump into the framework  I'd like to provide a little bit more  context talk a little bit about what  data is and how I think about it so data  is basically just encoded information  and information is everywhere everything  that you know everything that you can  think of everything that you encounter  every moment of every day has the  potential to be captured encoded and  therefore turned into data and this  makes our world a very complex web of it  and so we can think of it each data set  that we encounter in our work as just a  slice of this complexity that someone  has put together to communicate  something about the world to either a  human or a machine and so now as humans  we have this built-in ability to deal  with this complexity all around us and  all this information that we're  constantly receiving and the way that we  do this is by organizing things right we  put things in order we group things into  categories we create hierarchical  classifications taxonomies we tag things  now all that stuff and these things help  provide order in structure to the way  that we view the world and they allow us  to be able to take a look at something  recognize the similarities it has to a  group of other things and then identify  its properties it has so that we can be  able to make decisions about it and so  the fact is this ability to create kind  of order out of complexity is built-in  to us is great and we use it all the  time really without ever really thinking  about it and unfortunately that also  includes when we're analyzing data so I  wanted to put together a data  exploration framework that took  advantage of this ability in US and that  would help us make better use of it in  our workflows  and this is what I came up with so the  framework is basically broken down into  two phases  there's the prep phase on top and then  you've got the explore phase and  throughout the presentation we're going  to walk through each of the steps here  and I will point out that you can  technically do the explore phase without  the prep phase but it's really the prep  phase that's going to allow you to do  the exploration well and so I highly  recommend taking the time to kind of go  through the entire thing all right so  the data set that I'm going to be  applying this framework to today is the  Environmental Protection Agency's  vehicle fuel economy data set and I've  included the URL there so anybody that  wants to download it couldn't download  it and this is what the data set looks  like after some flight cleanup all right  and so jumping right into the framework  here the first step in the preface was  to identify a few different things about  our data set so let's go ahead and do  that the first thing that we're going to  identify is the types of information  that are contained in our data and so  just from taking a look at this there's  a few kind of obvious types of  information that really jump right out  at us  we've got vehicle attribute information  in there we've got vehicle manufacturer  information we've got engine information  fuel information all these things right  there's also some additional kind of  maybe not so obvious types of  information that are in our data set so  since we've got the year in there that  each vehicle was made we can take a look  at changes in the data over time in  addition to that there's also  relationship and information in our data  set both in terms of the relationships  between fields and also between entities  and because we have both time and  relationship information we can acquire  information about how those  relationships change over time as well  all right so the next step is to  identify the actual entities that are in  our data and so what exactly do I mean  when I say entities so when I refer to  an entity I'm talking about the  individual analyzable units that are in  a data set in order to conduct the  analysis the entities must be  distinguishable from one another right a  human or a computer needs to be able to  look at them and say this is one this is  another etc and finally entities you  usually have a hierarchical structure  where they're usually able to be  aggregated into the system or the  higher-level entity that they belong to  and so now that we've defined this let's  take a look at the entities in our data  set so here we're starting off at level  one which is the most granular that we  have and that's the year and the  specific model of vehicle and now from  there we can aggregate that up and look  at the year and the model type which is  a little less granular and from there we  have a few different directions that we  can head we got the year and the vehicle  class the year and the make or we can  just strip the year away and keep the  model tied and then finally at level 4  we can aggregate even further to just  the the vehicle classes the years or  what makes to drive that home and  clarify it a little bit further here's  an actual example from the data set so  down at the bottom level which is the  year in the specific model you have a  2016 Ford Mustang with 2.3 liter v4  engine automatic transmission and  rear-wheel drive at level 2 we can roll  things up and take a look at just all  all 2016 Ford Mustangs as one entity  that we're analyzing and then at level 3  you can do the same thing kind of with  all 2016 subcompact cars all 2016 Ford's  or address all Ford Mustangs regardless  regardless of the year they were made  and then it's it's kind of up the  hierarchy from there  and and again so doing this is important  and it'll help you think about all the  different things that you can do with  your data and all the different ways  that you can look at it later on and I  see a lot of people that are new to data  science that don't do this right they  don't think like this and then they miss  things in the data that they should be  catching so I hope this helps to make  that a little bit clearer all right so  the next step in the prep phase is to  review some of the transformation and  visualization methods so that they're  fresh in our heads here and so first up  are the transformation methods so let's  take a look at some of my favorite ways  to transform data here so the first one  I've listed is is filtering which is  basically making the dataset smaller by  either looking at less rose less columns  or both the next one I've listed is  aggregation and disaggregation and this  is basically where you're changing the  levels at which you're analyzing the  data so you're getting either less or  more granular then we have pivoting  which is where you a great by more than  one variable along two axes so along the  rows and along the columns as well and  then the last one I have on here is  graph transformations which is where you  link the entities that you're analyzing  by the things that they have in common  and then examine the connections between  the entities and how they're related to  each other and so by transforming the  data you're ultimately altering the  structure of it which lets you take a  look at it from a bunch of different  perspectives and just like looking at  anything else from several different  perspectives you're gonna learn  something new about it from every way  that you look at it and the awesome  thing about that is that the number of  ways that we can transform the data is  only limited really by our creativity  and our imagination and so this for me  is one of the most exciting things about  working with data right all all the  different things you can do to it and  all the creative ways that you can  transform it  in addition to transforming the data I  also like to go a step further and  visualize it sometimes the  transformations that you perform can be  kind of difficult to interpret visually  and I find that plotting really allows  you to bring the human visual cortex  into your analytical process and usually  makes the insights jump right out at you  and so transformation and visualization  really go hand-in-hand since there's a  multitude of ways to transform the data  there's also a bunch of ways that you  can visualize it I personally like to  keep things fairly easy and so here are  a few of the visualizations that I use  most often and if you you don't know  what a few of these are there's tons of  pictures all right so the last step in  the prep phase is a little more involved  and that's to create additional  categories additional categorical fields  that are going to make our data easier  to transform in it and explore later on  and we're going to do this in three  different ways the first way is  identifying opportunities to create  higher level categories out of the  categorical variables that we currently  have and so imagine here aggregating by  transmission which has 44 unique  categories in our data set and trying to  look at that and analyze that it's going  to be tough and you can have to work  really hard to find any kind of insights  there but if we take a look at the  different categories and find things  that some of them have in common then we  can make it a little bit easier on  ourselves and so in this case the thing  that all 44 values had in common is that  they fall into one of two categories  right either automatic transmission or a  manual transmission and the same thing  with the vehicle class field in our data  set we started off with 34 different  categorical values but I was able to  distill those down into eight categories  which is a lot easier to look at okay so  keep in mind that when you're doing this  you're not overriding the original  categories what you're doing is you're  creating another categorical field  that's allowing you to view the  information at a higher level and so if  you need to drill  to the more granular categories you can  always do that but at least now that  you've done this we have a choice the  next way that that we can create  additional categories for ourselves is  by bidding our continuous variables so  breaking them up into different  categories based on how high or how low  they are and there's um there's there's  multiple ways that you can do this but I  like to use quintiles because it gives  me one middle category and then two  categories that outside of that which  are moderately higher and lower and then  two extreme categories at the ends and I  just find that that's a very kind of an  intuitive way to take a look at things  and to split things up and so here I've  identified four fields in our data that  we could do this with and I've created  quintiles for each one and the final  thing that we're gonna do in this phase  is cluster to create categories out of  the different clusters that the  algorithm finds so there's a few reasons  that I like to use clustering for  creating categories specifically so  first it takes multiple fields into  consideration together whereas the other  kind of categorization methods that we  just looked at only consider one field  at a time it also creates new categories  for you automatically which saves time  versus you having to comb through the  data trying to find patterns then you  can form categories on this will  automatically group all the similar  items together for you and the third  reason I like clustering is because it  sometimes groups things in ways that you  as a human made out may not have thought  of right and I'm generally a big fan of  humans and machines working together but  the question with clustering is always  well how many clusters right and the  answer is usually it depends there's no  real like right answer for this and so  that being said for this purpose I  usually like to keep it to under ten  because otherwise it becomes kind of too  many categories for you to take a look  at and intuitively like distinguish  between and when you're clustering to  create meaningful categories it's key  that you're able to distinguish one from  the other  just like the entities that we discussed  earlier  and so I usually start out with about  eight or ten clusters and then work my  way down until I find some clear  boundaries between the clusters so you  should be able to look at them and  visually distinguish one from the others  and so starting out that's going to look  like this right so I've basically ran  the numerical fields in our data through  a k-means clustering algorithm and had  it group the data into eight clusters  and so just from looking at this you can  tell that we probably have too many  clusters here because clusters three  four and six look a lot alike clusters 0  and 1 are also pretty close and clusters  2 and 5 also look a lot alike as well  and so just judging from the way this  looks I'm willing to bet that we can cut  the number of clusters in half and get  better boundaries so let's take a look  at that all right so here we've reduced  the number of clusters down to 4 and now  the clusters look a lot better don't  they so cluster 0 &amp; 1 kind of look like  polar opposites of each other cluster 2  looks like it's pretty well balanced all  the way across the board and cluster 3  looks like it's kind of halfway between  clusters 0 and cluster 2 and so now we  have a good and so so now we have a good  number of clusters right but it's kind  of hard to remember what clusters 0 1 2  &amp; 3 mean and so the next step I like to  take is to assign descriptive names to  each of the clusters and so here's based  on the field that we were using to  cluster I basically just assigned some  descriptive cluster names to each one  and so now that we have these meaningful  categories our data is in really good  shape and that means that we can move on  to the explore phase  so in this explore phase we're going to  first look at filtering and aggregation  and then we're going to look at  relationships between fields and after  that we're going to move on and look on  look to relationships between the  entities in our data we're going to look  at these things visually we're going to  look at these things over time whenever  possible and we're going to use them to  discover insights in our data and so to  start out here I filtered the data by  year so just for 2016 and I've  aggregated it by vehicle type and here's  what kind of the transformation looks  like and then be accompanying bar chart  and so from this we can see that there's  there's currently significantly more  small cars being made than all the other  cars followed by SUVs and then midsize  cars after that and so once you've done  this once it's pretty easy to change out  the year and get a different historical  perspective and so here's the exact same  aggregation for 1985 this time and so  here  SUV didn't exist yet small cars are even  more popular as we're pickup trucks as  well and and then the next one with  special purpose vehicles which is things  like ambulances ice cream trucks that  kind of thing and so in addition to  being able to change the year here you  can also change out the field that  you're grouping on and so here we come  back to the current year and I plugged  in the engine size quintiles that we  created earlier in the prep phase and I  can do the same thing with the fuel  efficiency categories that we created or  even with the clusters and this is great  but what if we wanted to take a look at  the vehicle manufacturers right let's  say we wanted to know how many models  each manufacturer makes or even which  manufacturer currently has the most  models out there well we can do that as  well by just plugging in the make field  and so here we can see the BMW currently  has more models on the market than all  the other auto manufacturers and by  quite a bit actually  all right so so far we've been looking  at the data at a pretty high level  aggregating up by just a single  categorical variable at a time next  we're gonna go down a level and take a  look at two or more variables at a time  and the way that we're gonna do that is  with pivot tables and so here I've  created a pivot heat map where you have  a row for each fuel efficiency category  and a column for each engine size  category and you can see how many  vehicles fall into each combination of  these and now just like we did earlier  we can slice this by a different year  and easily get a different perspective  so here's what the heat map looks like  for 1985 and so with this approach we're  not just limited to two variables we can  have multiple variables for any of the  axes and it'll show all the different  combinations of values for those  variables by whatever other variables we  want and so in this heat map I've  doubled up on the rows I've got the  engine size and the fuel efficiency  combinations represented by the rows and  then we've added a third variable the  vehicle category across the columns on  the bottom there and so now you can see  a finer level of detail about what types  of cards have what size engines and what  level of fuel efficiency we've added a  little bit more complexity and a little  bit more granularity to how we're  viewing the data here and so if we  wanted to go back to analyzing the  different manufacturers what we can do  is take a look at how the number of  bottles they have on the market is  distributed across the different vehicle  categories so kind of kind of something  like this and now I've got a better  sense of each cup company's current  strategy in terms of the breadth versus  the depth of vehicles that they make so  by taking a look at this we can see that  like the BMW is making a bunch of small  cars more small cars than any other  category and Porsche is also Ford Chevy  and Nissan are kind of going for breadth  they make vehicles in almost every  category Jeep and Land Rover are making  only SUVs Ram is only making pickup  trucks there's a few other companies  that are  only making small cars and then all the  other companies are kind of somewhere in  between there alright so in addition to  looking at these different aggregations  and pivots visually at a given point in  time I also find it very useful to take  a look at how they've changed over time  and so that's what we're gonna do here  next so for example here are our vehicle  categories over time so we can see from  this that small cars are generally  dominated across the board we can also  see the introduction and increase in  popularity of SUV starting in about the  late 90s and we can see the gradual  decline in popularity of pickup trucks  as well and so now from here if we  wanted to we could zoom in and filter  for a specific manufacturer in order to  see how their specific strategy is has  has changed over the years and so here's  the same chart for BMW their vehicle  categories over time and we can see that  they started off making a reasonable  number of small cars and then sometime  in the late 90s they made a decision to  start increasing the types of small cars  that they make and we can contrast that  with a company like Toyota for example  who started out making a lot of small  cars and then made a decision kind of  around the same time  to start making less and less of them  over the years instead shifting their  focus to SUVs and pickup trucks  all right so that's the filter and  aggregate stage the next step in the  explore phase is examining the  relationships between fields in our data  so for looking at the relationships  between variables I like to start off  with a scatter matrix and this kind of  gives me a bird's eye view of all the  relationships and all the fields in my  data set so from this we can see that  there's some kind of more linear  relationships in there this there's also  some more exponential ones as well and  this is great but what's even better is  that we can add another dimension of  insight to this by overlaying a layer of  colors on top of this that represents  for example the clusters that we created  earlier and so here I've overlaid our  clusters and now we can see not only the  relationships but also where the  different cluster boundaries are and how  much overlap there is from one cluster  to the next and once we've got this  colorful bird's-eye view now we can  easily zoom in on anything that looks  interesting to us and so here I've  zoomed in on the engine size versus the  fuel efficiency fields  what the cluster is overlaid and so from  this we can see kind of our very small  are a small very efficient cluster on  the upper left in the purple there  followed by our small moderately  efficient cluster in blue and then our  midsize balanced cluster in red and  finally our large inefficient cluster at  the bottom right there in green and so  here I've clustered the engine size  against the fuel cost so more of a  linear relationship with the clusters  and here the clusters kind of follow the  same progression but this time from the  bottom left to the top right and again  you can do this for any combination of  variables that you had in the scatter  matrix  all right so the third step in our  explore phase is examining relationships  between entities in our data set and the  way that we're going to do that is  through graph analysis so as I said  before graph analysis is gonna allow us  to explore the relationships between  entities and these relationships are  usually based on attributes that the  entities have in common  so the entities we're going to analyze  in our example here is the auto  manufacturers and in this case the  attributes that they have in common are  going to be vehicles with similar  attributes so we're gonna examine how  the different manufacturers relate to  each other and how those relationships  have changed over time now in order to  do this we're gonna have to transform  our data set yet again from the  relational form it's currently in here  into graph form and that's gonna look  like this so here we have two different  companies as make X and make Y and then  we have an edge weight and the edge  weight tells us how many vehicles with  similar attributes the two companies  have in common and so the more vehicles  with similar attributes they have in  common the more alike that the two  companies are gonna be and the stronger  their connection is gonna be and so  looking at the data in this format you  might say there's not much to it and  you're right  but having it formatted like this lets  you create really interesting things  like this and so this is a network  visualization showing the relationships  of the different manufacturers to each  other and from taking a look at this we  can see for example that the companies  that make the most types of vehicles  across the most number of categories are  positioned towards the center of the  network and that's because they're gonna  have more in common with other companies  just as a result of the depth and  breadth of their offerings you can  contrast that with other companies that  have more specialized offerings like RAM  at the very top there or a Land Rover at  the bottom they only make one type of  vehicle and so they have less  connections and they're gonna be  positioned towards the edges so this is  great but taking a look at all this all  at once does make it look a little bit  cluttered and so what I usually like to  do from here  is to filter this down and take a look  at an ego got an ego graph for a  specific node and so I went ahead 