ady to move on to the  explore phase we started off by tuning  the filtrate an aggregate step taking a  look at the bar charts and the pivot  heat maps then we moved on to the  relationships between our fields and  then finally we use graph analysis to  analyze the relationship between our  entities that are in our data we did all  this visually we did all this over time  whenever possible and what do we end up  with at the end of the day so many  insights right I mean just in the short  amount of time that we've been taking a  look at this data set look at all the  insights that we've discovered from it  about one insight per minute on average  which is actually pretty good so we're  not doing too bad here so in wrapping up  I'd like to leave you with a few more  things to think about and in addition to  just the framework I hope that you find  the framework useful but there are a few  other important kind of more understated  things that I really like to call  attention to here at the end so the  first thing is I'd like to encourage you  not to take the data that you're giving  it at face value  right it's it's only really a starting  point and I hope that I've demonstrated  here that the more effort you put into  thinking about the data prepping the  data transforming the data and looking  at the data from different perspectives  the more valuable insights you're going  to find  the second thing I'd like you to  consider is whether or not something  like this can be automated or more  specifically which parts of this can be  automated and how can we do that so my  personal opinion is that there are  definitely some parts of the framework  that can be automated but there are also  other parts where it's very important  and very valuable to have a human and a  human's intuition in the mix there and  that brings me to the last point that I  want to stress and that's the importance  of humans and machines working together  to discover more insights in the data  than 